The EVO 3D Engine (v4.2) is highly sophisticated, specifically designed for high-performance, PBR-driven creative coding within the Folio Core runtime. It is not a "toy" engine—it is an ECS-based (Entity Component System) declarative engine that treats geometry, lighting, physics, and storyboard camera logic as first-class, serializable JSON data.

Here is a breakdown of its "advancement" levels:

1. Rendering Architecture
PBR (Physically Based Rendering): It uses a full PBR workflow with 14 high-fidelity material presets (glass, chrome, neon, obsidian, lava, ice, etc.) that respond dynamically to global environment lighting (environment: "cyberpunk" | "cosmic" | "studio", etc.).
Procedural Shading: Supports emissivePulse, iridescence, sheen, and flatShading. You can apply gradient patterns across any of the 29 supported geometries.
Atmospherics: Includes a global lighting rig, fog (density/color), starfield (up to 320 points), and adjustable camera exposure.
2. Physics & Kinematics
Rigid-Body Simulation: Built-in support for velocity, gravity, drag, restitution (bounciness), and attractor fields.
Collision Detection: You can attach hitbox definitions to any node. These are optimized for fast AABB (Axis-Aligned Bounding Box) or Sphere-based intersection calculations.
Motion Math: This is the most powerful part—you have access to 120+ math functions (including projectile, dampedSine, pendulum, orbit) and 36 easing curves (e.g., easeOutElastic, easeInOutCubic) that can be bound to any object's properties at runtime.
3. Storyboard & Camera System
Camera Sequencing: EVO is built for "shots." You can define an array of storyboard.shots that trigger dynamic camera cuts, position interpolations, or target shifts, making it perfect for both playable scenes and cinematic intros.
4. Why it fits your Game
If you are building a game in the Folio environment, EVO is significantly faster than using a generic engine because it is native-serialized.

