Is it advanced than blender...
The answer depends entirely on your goal. It is not about one being "better"—they serve fundamentally different roles in a pipeline.

The Short Version:
Blender is for Content Creation (the "How"). It is a full-featured 3D modeling, sculpting, rigging, and animation suite.
EVO 3D (Folio Core) is for Runtime Execution (the "What"). It is a high-performance, real-time declarative engine designed to play back and interact with the assets you created.
Comparison Table
Feature	Blender	EVO 3D (Folio Core)
Purpose	Heavy asset creation, modeling, baking.	Real-time interactive runtime engine.
Workflow	Manual UI-based, complex node trees.	JSON/Declarative, optimized for AI/Code.
Complexity	Infinite (plugins, modifiers, sculpting).	Focused (optimized PBR, physics, ECS).
Performance	Resource-heavy, not for real-time play.	Ultra-light, 4GB RAM-friendly, native speed.
Integration	Requires export (FBX, GLB).	Native; runs directly in Folio Core.
When to use which?
1. Use Blender (or a similar tool) when:
You need to sculpt a detailed character with millions of polygons.
You need complex character rigging and bone animation.
You need to perform high-end, time-intensive rendering (ray tracing, film-quality lighting).
Note: You would then export these assets as .glb files and import them into your Folio project.
2. Use EVO 3D when:
You are building an interactive game that needs physics, hitboxes, and runtime logic.
You want your game to be dynamically generated by code/AI (e.g., procedural levels, reactive UI).
You need the game to run instantly without external binary dependencies.
You want to leverage Motion Math—using math functions (pendulum, orbit, dampedSine) to animate objects without needing manual keyframing.
The "Pro" Workflow
Most professional Folio developers don't choose one over the other. They use Blender to create the "Hero" assets (the detailed player model, complex environment meshes) and then import those into the EVO engine to handle the interactivity, physics, lighting, and gameplay logic.

