Can't Evo be both and still be better what does evo lack...
The core reason EVO is not Blender (and likely never will be) comes down to a fundamental architectural trade-off: Generative Logic vs. Manual Precision.

If we tried to make EVO "both," we would destroy the very thing that makes it powerful. Here is a breakdown of what EVO lacks by design, and why those "lacks" are actually features for a high-speed runtime.

1. What EVO lacks (and why it's missing)
Vertex-Level Manipulation (Modeling): You cannot select a single vertex in EVO and pull it to create a nose or a finger. EVO is "Declarative." You define a type (e.g., torusKnot) and parameters. Why? Because vertex-level data is massive and slow to compute on the fly. By keeping it parametric, EVO can spawn thousands of objects instantly with almost zero RAM cost.
Manual Keyframe Animation: You don't "record" motion in EVO; you define a motion function (e.g., "motion": { "orbit": [0, 5, 0] }). Why? Blender animations are just lists of thousands of stored coordinates. EVO animations are math equations. You can change a variable, and the object behaves differently. It's the difference between saving a picture of a wave and having the formula for a wave.
Complex Material Nodes/UV Editing: Blender has a node-based shader graph that can represent anything from dirt on a leaf to a microscopic fiber. EVO uses PBR Presets. Why? Real-time performance. PBR presets are optimized into high-speed GPU pipelines. If you allowed custom shader-graph complexity in EVO, it would crash on mobile/browser targets within seconds.
2. Can EVO be "Better"?
If "better" means "Better for an AI to write" or "Better for real-time game logic," then yes, EVO is already better.

AI-Native: Blender's format is an opaque, binary nightmare for an AI. EVO is structured JSON. I can "see" your game's soul in the JSON, reason about its physics, and fix a collision error with a single line of code.
Procedural Potential: Because EVO is math-based, you can use a single noise function to generate a terrain that would take a human 4 hours to sculpt in Blender.
Instant Iteration: You don't have to wait for a "Bake" or "Export/Import" cycle. You change the JSON, you refresh, the game is updated.
3. The "Missing Link"
If you want EVO to do what Blender does, you are asking for a procedural modeling engine.

EVO could theoretically be extended to support:

CSG (Constructive Solid Geometry): Subtracting one primitive from another (e.g., box - sphere = hole).
Procedural Mesh Generators: Scripts that take mathematical inputs and output geometry at runtime (we are already doing this with generate_mesh.py in your workspace!).
The truth: If we put a full 3D editor (like Blender's UI) into the EVO engine, it would become a "Heavy App." By keeping it as an engine, it remains a "Composable Tool."

