List the ideas that can improve evo and describe
To take EVO 3D to the next level while maintaining its lightweight, AI-native philosophy, we don't need "more features"—we need "smarter" primitives.

Here are five conceptual improvements that would bridge the gap between a declarative engine and a professional game creator:

1. Neural Shader Injection
The Concept: Instead of selecting from 14 hard-coded presets, allow a small "latent space" vector to be passed in the material object.
The Improvement: A lightweight shader kernel would synthesize unique material textures at runtime based on the vector.
Why: You keep the file size tiny (a few floating-point numbers), but you get infinite visual variety (e.g., "a galaxy-obsidian hybrid") without needing high-res textures.
2. "Soft-Body" Physics via Vertex-Field Math
The Concept: Expand the physics component to support deformation math (e.g., squashAndStretch or wobble coefficients).
The Improvement: When an object hits a wall, instead of just bouncing, the engine applies a mathematical ripple across the vertex field based on the object's material.elasticity.
Why: It makes the game feel "alive" and tactile without requiring expensive complex mesh simulations.
3. Procedural "SDF" (Signed Distance Field) Primitives
The Concept: Add an sdf geometry type that accepts a custom function.
The Improvement: Instead of geometry: "box", you define geometry: "sdf_sphere + sdf_box". The engine renders the boolean result in real-time.
Why: This allows for seamless "blending" of shapes, creating complex organic forms that are impossible with standard static primitives.
4. Logic-Event Triggers (The "Action" Schema)
The Concept: Introduce an events array in the JSON schema.
The Improvement: Declarative triggers: "events": { "onCollision": "player_ship": "trigger_sound(hit_metallic)", "onDistance": { "target": "player", "range": 5, "action": "set_visible: true" } }.
Why: This removes the need to write complex boilerplate code in sdk/index.js, letting the AI handle game flow directly inside the model.evo.json file.
5. Multi-User "Ghost" Sync (Networking Protocol)
The Concept: A native sync tag within nodes.
The Improvement: When a node has "sync": true, the engine automatically pushes its JSON state (x, y, z, rotation) to a lightweight relay server.
Why: It makes the engine instantly multiplayer. Two people could have the same model.evo.json open, and they would see each other’s objects moving in real-time, essentially turning any game into a collaborative experience.
