Here is a comprehensive list of 20 ideas to evolve the EVO 3D Engine into a world-class creative platform. These draw inspiration from Blender’s professional toolset while maintaining the Folio Core's declarative, AI-native, and high-performance philosophy.

The Blender-Inspired "Evo Evolution" Roadmap (20 Ideas)
I. Procedural Modeling & Sculpting (Geometry)
SDF Primitive Blending (Metaballs): Inspired by Blender's Metaballs; allows runtime boolean blending of primitives into organic "blob" shapes.
Modifier Stack System: Inspired by Blender's Modifier Stack; adds a list of non-destructive operations (Subdivision Surface, Array, Mirror, Bevel) to any mesh node.
Lattice Deformers: Inspired by Lattice modifiers; allows an outer "cage" to manipulate the mesh vertices of a character or object.
Geometry Nodes (Schema): A JSON-based node graph that allows chaining procedural operations (Scatter, Instance on Points, Randomize Transform).
Vertex Painting: Support for local color data per vertex, allowing for custom blending, dirt mapping, and highlights without heavy texture files.
II. Animation & Rigging
Constraint System: Inspired by Blender Constraints (Copy Rotation, Track To, Limit Distance); forces nodes to interact based on sibling relationships.
IK (Inverse Kinematics) JSON: Defines a "chain" of nodes where moving a "hand" node automatically solves the arm/shoulder positioning via math.
Morph Targets (Shape Keys): Allows defining a "base" mesh and "target" meshes; linearly interpolates between them (e.g., for facial expressions).
Action Strip Sequences: A non-linear animation editor that blends multiple motion JSON snippets (e.g., "Run" + "Wave" = "Running while waving").
Bezier Path Follow: Allows any object to be locked to a 3D curve (Bezier) defined in the scene graph for complex flight paths.
III. Materials & Rendering (The PBR Pipeline)
Node-Based Shader Graph: An abstraction of Blender’s Shader Editor; allows combining mathematical noise patterns, gradients, and emissive maps at runtime.
Screen Space Ambient Occlusion (SSAO): Adds depth-based shadows to crevices, making the game look instantly "photorealistic" compared to basic primitives.
Dynamic Decals: Allows projecting 2D textures or symbols onto 3D surfaces at runtime (like impact marks or graffiti).
Custom Reflection Probes: Allows defining "mirror" zones that capture the local surroundings to provide accurate environmental reflections on chrome/glass.
IV. Physics & Interactivity (Gameplay Logic)
Soft-Body Constraints: Inspired by Blender’s Soft-Body sims; introduces "tension" and "dampening" to meshes for cloth or jelly-like physics.
Collision "Trigger Zones": A high-level object type that executes a code block when a player object enters its 3D boundary.
Attractor/Repulsor Fields: Allows nodes to emit force fields that affect the velocity of other physics-enabled objects nearby.
Ragdoll Physics System: Allows a multi-node character chain to go limp and react to forces (e.g., explosions or falls) upon a "death" trigger.